1
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:17,620
Welcome back everyone, I hope you are ready to
dive into today's lesson and learn something new.

2
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:23,140
Today we're going to learn about a quite
challenging question type in the IELTS reading test.

3
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:25,300
True, false or not given.

4
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:29,540
However, don't worry guys, today we're going to tackle it together.

5
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:37,880
This lesson aims to help you understand and identify
features of true, false or not given questions and answers.

6
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:42,580
Understand and apply steps for true, false or not given questions.

7
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:45,980
Alright, no more delay, let's begin.

8
00:00:50,810 --> 00:00:55,430
First of all, let's explore the features
of the true, false or not given questions.

9
00:00:55,910 --> 00:01:00,910
This question type often appears in reading passage 1 and 2 of the test.

10
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:06,050
The thing that helps you identify this question type is the question, do the

11
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:09,590
following statements agree with the information given in the reading passage?

12
00:01:10,470 --> 00:01:14,690
So this question type requires candidates to identify whether statements in

13
00:01:14,690 --> 00:01:20,290
questions agree or disagree with the information
in the reading passage or are not mentioned.

14
00:01:20,870 --> 00:01:25,470
Let's take a closer look at an example to
understand the structure of this question type.

15
00:01:26,910 --> 00:01:28,770
The question type has two parts.

16
00:01:29,570 --> 00:01:34,090
The first one is the requirements requiring candidates to identify whether

17
00:01:34,090 --> 00:01:36,970
given statements are true, false or not given.

18
00:01:37,930 --> 00:01:40,370
The second one is the numbered statements.

19
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:44,990
For this question type, there is a feature that can be considered as an

20
00:01:44,990 --> 00:01:47,910
advantage and helps us handle it more quickly.

21
00:01:48,910 --> 00:01:53,810
It is, these statements come in order as the information in the passage.

22
00:01:55,170 --> 00:01:59,610
This means that if we find the information for statement 1 in the previous line

23
00:01:59,610 --> 00:02:04,070
or paragraph, we can move on to the next line or paragraph to find the

24
00:02:04,070 --> 00:02:05,610
information for the next statement.

25
00:02:06,170 --> 00:02:11,150
So for this question type, finding the
information isn't really time consuming, is it?

26
00:02:11,730 --> 00:02:17,270
Next, we're going to learn how to identify
which information is true, false or not given.

27
00:02:17,730 --> 00:02:23,510
If we can't distinguish these three types of answers,
we won't be able to choose the correct answer.

28
00:02:24,350 --> 00:02:27,850
Distinguish three types of answers, true, false or not given.

29
00:02:28,590 --> 00:02:29,550
1. True.

30
00:02:30,290 --> 00:02:34,610
The statement 100% agrees with the information in the passage.

31
00:02:35,370 --> 00:02:40,310
Firstly, the statement will be true if it 100%
agrees with the information in the passage.

32
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:44,590
You should note that the statement should 100% agree with the conveyed

33
00:02:44,590 --> 00:02:50,670
information because the keywords in the statement
might not 100% match those of the passage.

34
00:02:51,430 --> 00:02:54,070
Instead, the keywords might be paraphrased.

35
00:02:54,670 --> 00:02:58,490
Speaking of paraphrasing, does anyone remember the techniques for paraphrasing

36
00:02:58,490 --> 00:02:59,590
that I introduced to you?

37
00:03:00,330 --> 00:03:04,530
In case you don't, I'm going to introduce
some techniques for paraphrasing again.

38
00:03:05,110 --> 00:03:09,650
We have four techniques which are called GOSS for short.

39
00:03:10,170 --> 00:03:10,550
Grammar.

40
00:03:11,290 --> 00:03:14,810
Express the same information using different grammatical structures.

41
00:03:15,970 --> 00:03:21,250
Example, change the word form or the active voice to the passive voice.

42
00:03:21,830 --> 00:03:24,410
The first one is paraphrasing using grammar.

43
00:03:25,230 --> 00:03:28,690
For example, keywords can be changed in terms of word form.

44
00:03:29,250 --> 00:03:32,710
Original sentence can be changed from active voice to passive voice.

45
00:03:33,390 --> 00:03:33,490
Order.

46
00:03:34,290 --> 00:03:37,430
Reversing the order of some information in the sentence.

47
00:03:38,010 --> 00:03:43,070
The second technique is called ORDER, which reverses the information order of

48
00:03:43,070 --> 00:03:44,610
some information in the sentence.

49
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,390
We can encounter this technique in sentences that have a feature of listing.

50
00:03:51,090 --> 00:03:51,450
Summary.

51
00:03:52,290 --> 00:03:55,730
Use a collective noun instead of listing all the items.

52
00:03:56,390 --> 00:04:01,950
The third one is summary, in which we use a
collective noun instead of listing all the items.

53
00:04:03,370 --> 00:04:03,730
Synonym.

54
00:04:04,570 --> 00:04:09,390
Use words, phrases or expressions that have
similar meanings to the original sentence.

55
00:04:10,130 --> 00:04:14,270
The final technique is synonym, in which we use words, phrases or expressions

56
00:04:14,270 --> 00:04:17,810
that have a similar meaning to the original sentence.

57
00:04:18,510 --> 00:04:18,790
False.

58
00:04:19,410 --> 00:04:22,450
The statement contradicts the information in the passage.

59
00:04:23,490 --> 00:04:27,210
The statement will be false if it contradicts the information in the passage.

60
00:04:28,230 --> 00:04:32,130
So for false statements, the structures are often small details.

61
00:04:32,890 --> 00:04:34,950
Therefore, I have a tip for you.

62
00:04:35,650 --> 00:04:41,950
Pay attention to details, especially qualifying words, such as Adverbs of

63
00:04:41,950 --> 00:04:49,570
frequency, like often, barely, always, and sometimes Adverbs of probability,

64
00:04:50,110 --> 00:04:58,570
like likely and probably Quantifiers, like some, many, and a few Adverbs

65
00:04:58,570 --> 00:05:08,670
indicating exclusivity, like purely, solely, only Unique, and mainly Modal

66
00:05:08,670 --> 00:05:18,690
verbs, like can, could, should, and must
Ordinal numbers, like first, second, third, etc.

67
00:05:19,930 --> 00:05:27,070
Adjectives of time order, like newest, latest, most recent, or oldest

68
00:05:27,070 --> 00:05:32,330
These words and phrases can completely change the
meaning of the question when compared to the passage.

69
00:05:32,750 --> 00:05:35,370
So pay attention to them to avoid the distractors.

70
00:05:36,050 --> 00:05:40,030
Finally, we're going to learn about the final type of answer that is the most

71
00:05:40,030 --> 00:05:41,350
confusing to the candidates.

72
00:05:41,970 --> 00:05:42,550
Not given.

73
00:05:42,990 --> 00:05:43,650
Not given.

74
00:05:44,210 --> 00:05:46,790
There is no information on the passage.

75
00:05:47,270 --> 00:05:51,190
This is a difficult type of answer because candidates don't know what

76
00:05:51,190 --> 00:05:54,390
information to find or what information not to find.

77
00:05:55,110 --> 00:06:00,850
Also, it can take them a lot of time to identify
whether the statement is false or not given.

78
00:06:01,430 --> 00:06:04,310
So what is the most important feature of not given statements?

79
00:06:04,830 --> 00:06:08,990
To understand this, we're going to analyze the example in the funny skit.

80
00:06:09,690 --> 00:06:14,790
In 20 seconds, you'll read the following not given statement and guess which

81
00:06:14,790 --> 00:06:18,450
part in the statement is not mentioned in the information area.

82
00:06:18,870 --> 00:06:21,070
20 seconds, start now.

83
00:06:43,410 --> 00:06:44,350
Have you found the answer?

84
00:06:44,690 --> 00:06:49,990
Although the statement includes keywords like bamboo, grow, and grass that are

85
00:06:49,990 --> 00:06:53,430
similar to the topic of the passage, there is a crucial difference.

86
00:06:53,790 --> 00:06:57,510
The passage certainly talks about how fast bamboo grows.

87
00:06:57,870 --> 00:07:04,150
However, it does not mention a comparison regarding
how difficult it is to grow compared to other plants.

88
00:07:04,530 --> 00:07:07,030
It never claims it is the most difficult.

89
00:07:07,570 --> 00:07:11,630
Therefore, a statement is not given when it contains keywords or their

90
00:07:11,630 --> 00:07:16,390
paraphrases from the passage, but the specific relationship or comparison found

91
00:07:16,390 --> 00:07:18,790
in the statement is not mentioned in the text.

92
00:07:20,470 --> 00:07:25,410
It sounds quite simple, but students still mistake not given for true or false.

93
00:07:26,050 --> 00:07:29,370
So why do we easily make mistakes with not given statements?

94
00:07:29,910 --> 00:07:33,750
Let's watch the funny skit again and identify two mistakes that make the

95
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:35,510
character choose the wrong answer.

96
00:07:35,730 --> 00:07:38,630
The first common mistake is relying on personal knowledge.

97
00:07:39,030 --> 00:07:42,850
For example, you might look at this and think, well, I tried to grow bamboo

98
00:07:42,850 --> 00:07:45,370
once and it died, so it must be difficult.

99
00:07:45,690 --> 00:07:48,730
This assumption leads you to choose true incorrectly.

100
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:53,570
When taking the actual test, familiar topics can help us understand the

101
00:07:53,570 --> 00:07:58,210
content, but we must never choose an answer based on our own outside knowledge.

102
00:07:58,570 --> 00:08:03,030
We must strictly compare the statement with
the information provided in the passage.

103
00:08:04,450 --> 00:08:07,750
The second common mistake is relying only on keywords.

104
00:08:08,430 --> 00:08:12,990
You might see that bamboo and grow are present in both the text and the

105
00:08:12,990 --> 00:08:17,470
statement, so you immediately assume the answer
is true without checking the full meaning.

106
00:08:17,470 --> 00:08:22,210
By doing this, you miss the fact that the
comparison is missing from the text entirely.

107
00:08:22,670 --> 00:08:27,090
Common mistakes include relying on personal knowledge, relying on keywords.

108
00:08:27,510 --> 00:08:31,270
From these mistakes, what should we do to avoid choosing the wrong answer?

109
00:08:32,669 --> 00:08:34,450
Truly understand the meaning.

110
00:08:34,870 --> 00:08:38,929
Analyze the full content of the statement and the relevant part of the passage,

111
00:08:39,230 --> 00:08:40,770
not just the matching keywords.

112
00:08:41,270 --> 00:08:42,230
Stick to the text.

113
00:08:42,630 --> 00:08:46,670
Do not use personal knowledge to make assumptions or fill in gaps.

114
00:08:46,670 --> 00:08:48,270
Watch for qualifiers.

115
00:08:48,690 --> 00:08:55,530
Pay close attention to comparison words or qualifying
words that might be in the statement but not in the text.

116
00:08:55,890 --> 00:09:02,850
Alright, now that we've understood the features of this question
type, let's move on to part two to learn the steps for it.

117
00:09:08,490 --> 00:09:12,090
To tackle this question type, we need to follow these three steps.

118
00:09:12,950 --> 00:09:18,010
Step one, underline keywords in statements and pay attention to scanning words.

119
00:09:18,710 --> 00:09:22,910
Scanning words are keywords which are found more easily than other keywords,

120
00:09:23,410 --> 00:09:28,790
including unchangeable keywords like proper nouns, acronyms and numbers,

121
00:09:29,490 --> 00:09:32,550
compounds and long words.

122
00:09:33,790 --> 00:09:40,150
Step two, scan passages to locate information
areas containing keywords and their paraphrases.

123
00:09:41,370 --> 00:09:46,930
Step three, read information areas identified in step two carefully to decide

124
00:09:46,930 --> 00:09:49,930
whether these statements are true, false or not given.

125
00:09:50,850 --> 00:09:52,450
Here is a note for you.

126
00:09:53,270 --> 00:09:59,790
You should read the whole statement to understand its
meaning and shouldn't rely completely on keywords.

127
00:10:00,210 --> 00:10:03,530
Now that we have mastered the theory, let's put it into practice.

128
00:10:03,750 --> 00:10:07,610
We are going to apply the three steps we just learned to solve a specific

129
00:10:07,610 --> 00:10:11,370
question based on a reading passage about regulating hate speech.

130
00:10:11,650 --> 00:10:13,150
Let's look at question one.

131
00:10:13,390 --> 00:10:18,870
The United Nations considers any communication
that is simply rude to be a form of hate speech.

132
00:10:20,310 --> 00:10:24,770
Step one, underline keywords in statements and pay attention to scanning words.

133
00:10:25,090 --> 00:10:27,950
First, we need to identify the important words.

134
00:10:28,370 --> 00:10:33,030
Here, United Nations is a proper noun with capital letters, making it an

135
00:10:33,030 --> 00:10:36,510
excellent scanning word because it will be easy to spot in the text.

136
00:10:36,830 --> 00:10:39,170
We also need to underline the content keywords.

137
00:10:39,470 --> 00:10:42,730
Any communication, simply rude and hate speech.

138
00:10:43,070 --> 00:10:44,750
These determine the meaning of the sentence.

139
00:10:46,290 --> 00:10:49,850
Step two, scan passages to locate information areas.

140
00:10:50,210 --> 00:10:54,170
Now, move your eyes quickly over the passage to find United Nations.

141
00:10:54,630 --> 00:10:55,650
Hmm, there it is.

142
00:10:55,950 --> 00:10:57,790
It appears right in the first paragraph.

143
00:10:58,210 --> 00:10:59,510
This is our information area.

144
00:11:00,930 --> 00:11:05,230
Step three, read information areas carefully to decide the answer.

145
00:11:05,730 --> 00:11:08,070
Now we need to compare the statement with the text.

146
00:11:08,070 --> 00:11:13,950
The statement says, the UN considers communication
that is simply rude to be hate speech.

147
00:11:14,370 --> 00:11:20,210
The text says, hate speech is any kind of communication
that attacks or uses discriminatory language.

148
00:11:20,750 --> 00:11:24,510
Experts agree that hate speech is not just about being mean.

149
00:11:26,150 --> 00:11:27,210
Let's analyze this.

150
00:11:27,670 --> 00:11:29,510
Mean and rude are synonyms.

151
00:11:29,890 --> 00:11:35,290
The text explicitly says, hate speech is not just
about being mean, it requires discriminatory language.

152
00:11:35,290 --> 00:11:38,770
Therefore, the statement contradicts the information in the passage.

153
00:11:39,230 --> 00:11:41,350
So the answer for question one is false.

154
00:11:41,710 --> 00:11:45,510
See, by following these steps, we can avoid being tricked by words that look

155
00:11:45,510 --> 00:11:47,190
similar but have different meanings.

156
00:11:52,700 --> 00:11:56,600
Before finishing the lesson, we're going
to quickly recap what we learned today.

157
00:11:57,320 --> 00:12:04,860
After this lesson, we can understand and identify features
of true, false or not given questions and answers.

158
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:09,760
Understand and apply steps for true, false or not given questions.

159
00:12:10,820 --> 00:12:12,060
Three steps are.

160
00:12:12,700 --> 00:12:17,420
Step one, underline keywords in statements and pay attention to scanning words.

161
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:24,620
Step two, scan passages to locate information
areas containing keywords and their paraphrases.

162
00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:31,480
Step three, read information areas identified in step two carefully to decide

163
00:12:31,480 --> 00:12:34,220
whether these statements are true, false or not given.

164
00:12:35,480 --> 00:12:39,860
To master them, you need to do practice exercises for this question type.

165
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:42,220
For now, let's call it a day.

166
00:12:42,780 --> 00:12:44,120
See you in the next lesson.

167
00:12:44,740 --> 00:12:45,160
Goodbye.

